.TH STYLE 9
.SH NAME
style \- choose what a relief looks like
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f5style\fP ?\f5classic\fP|\f5flat\fP?
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Style
sets, for the whole toplevel, what a
.B -relief
means when it is drawn.
With no argument it reports which is in force.
.TP
\f5classic\fP
Every relief is a two-tone chisel: a light edge and a dark edge, so that
.B raised
stands out of the surface and
.B sunken
is cut into it.
This is the default, and it is the look this toolkit has always had.
.TP
\f5flat\fP
Every relief is one restrained outline of the widget's border width, in
the darker of the two shades.
.B Raised
and
.B sunken
draw the same thing;
.B flat
still draws nothing.
.PP
It is a property of the toplevel rather than an option on each widget,
and deliberately so.
What makes a set of widgets look like one set is that they agree, and an
option each widget could set differently is an option each program would
set differently.
.PP
Collapsing
.B raised
and
.B sunken
loses no information a person could act on, because every state that
uses a relief to show itself also changes the fill behind it: a pressed
button is drawn in
.BR activebgnd ,
and a
.IR checkbutton (9)
or
.IR radiobutton (9)
with
.B -indicatoron
false is drawn in
.B select
when it is chosen.
The relief was reinforcing those, not carrying them.
.PP
A style also picks the spacings a widget insets its contents by \- the
padding around a label's or button's text, around an image in a label,
inside an
.IR entry (9),
and inside a
.IR text (9).
The
.B flat
row is roomier than
.BR classic ,
and makes the label and text insets agree, which they did not: they were
separate constants in separate files that happened to differ by a pixel.
.PP
Changing the style repaints every widget in the toplevel, since every
border is drawn from this.
The spacings, though, are read when a widget is
.IR configured ,
so they apply to widgets built after the command and not to ones already
on the screen \- set the style before building the interface.
.PP
The
.B flat
style rounds a widget's corners, painting what falls outside the curve
back to the parent's background, and derives its default border width
from the font, so
that a line which reads as an edge at one size is not a hairline at
another.
It is the TOPLEVEL's font that decides it, not the widget's: the default
is set when the object is made, before its own
.B -font
has been parsed.
An explicit
.B -borderwidth
is never scaled.
Under
.B classic
the border is the literal number it always was, because every existing
layout has measured itself against that.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
style flat
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR options (9),
.IR frame (9),
.IR button (9),
.IR wmtheme (2)
.SH BUGS
A
.B style
command does not resize the widgets already on the screen, only
restyles their borders, so a program that changes style late gets flat
borders with classic spacings.
This is deliberate rather than unfinished.
Re-measuring them works, and was tried: the new size request travels up
to the master and on to the toplevel, which re-fits itself to its
contents, so a single
.B style
command resized the whole window and moved every widget in it.
A window that jumps size because its borders were restyled is a worse
thing than a style that applies its spacings to what is built after it.
.PP
It changes borders, spacings and corners.
The colours are what else would have to move for the two styles to look
like two designs rather than one design redrawn, and they are not here.
They already have a register of their own in
.IR wmtheme (2),
which nothing yet connects to this.
.PP
.IR Scale (9),
.IR scrollbar (9)
and the indicators of
.IR checkbutton (9)
and
.IR radiobutton (9)
draw their own chrome rather than going through
.IR tkdrawrelief ,
so each asks the style for itself with
.IR tkstyleflat .
Under
.B flat
a scale is a thin track filled to the knob in the accent with a round
knob above it; a scrollbar is a capsule in the trough with solid arrow
glyphs; a checkbutton is an empty box when off and a filled one with a
light tick when on; a radiobutton is a ring, filled when chosen.
Their GEOMETRY is unchanged in every case \- an arrow occupies the same
triangle, a thumb the same rectangle \- so the regions a click lands in
are exactly what they were, and only the paint differs.
.PP
A
.I frame
used purely as a divider, with a relief and no fill of its own, says
less in the flat style than in the classic one, because there the chisel
was the whole of what it drew.
.PP
There is no way to set it for a session, only per toplevel, so a program
that wants it must say so itself.
